(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-pharm-exam-1-master-exam-form-h"]={"title":"Pharmacology I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form H","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Besides hypertension, what is clonidine used for?","choices":["Withdrawal from opiates, tobacco and benzodiazepines","Acute asthma exacerbation","Benign prostatic hyperplasia with poor stream","Open-angle glaucoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, by minimising withdrawal symptoms. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"Why is propranolol contraindicated in asthma?","choices":["It causes pulmonary oedema directly","It triggers an allergic reaction in the airway","It reduces respiratory drive centrally","Beta-2 blockade causes bronchoconstriction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is why cardioselective agents exist. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"What does cefepime not cover?","choices":["Resistant Staphylococcus, Enterococcus, anaerobes","Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Enterobacter","Streptococcus pneumoniae and other streptococci","Escherichia coli and Klebsiella pneumoniae"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, despite its broad Gram-negative and Gram-positive reach. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What is griseofulvin ineffective against?","choices":["Trichophyton species","Microsporum species","Epidermophyton species","Candida"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It works on dermatophytes and is fungistatic. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"What were nafcillin, oxacillin and dicloxacillin designed to cover?","choices":["Methicillin-sensitive Staphylococcus aureus","Pseudomonas aeruginosa in hospital infection","Enterococcus and Listeria monocytogenes","Anaerobes including Bacteroides fragilis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with decreased activity against other organisms as the trade-off. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"How does stress contribute to acne?","choices":["It increases glucocorticoid secretion","It raises circulating histamine levels","It directly increases bacterial growth","It thins the stratum corneum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, listed as the physiological contributory factor. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which agent is given intravenously for a hypertensive emergency?","choices":["Labetalol","Carvedilol","Propranolol","Timolol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, blocking alpha-1 along with both beta receptors. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"Which agent was the first known competitive neuromuscular blocker?","choices":["Succinylcholine","Pancuronium","Rocuronium","Curare"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, purified as tubocurarine for clinical use in the 1940s. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Why start acne topicals at a lower strength?","choices":["Higher strengths are not manufactured","Most cause irritation and patients stop","Lower strengths work faster","Absorption is better at low concentration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, so strength is increased gradually as tolerated. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How do the azoles reduce ergosterol?","choices":["They inhibit lanosterol demethylase","They inhibit squalene epoxidase","They bind ergosterol already in the membrane","They inhibit 1,3-beta-D-glucan synthase"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, blocking conversion of lanosterol to ergosterol. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"Which topical corticosteroid is class 1 superpotent?","choices":["Hydrocortisone 1 percent cream","Clobetasol propionate 0.05 percent","Desonide 0.05 percent cream","Triamcinolone acetonide 0.1 percent cream"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, along with betamethasone dipropionate ointment. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"How do tacrolimus and pimecrolimus work?","choices":["They inhibit prostaglandin synthesis","They act as vitamin A derivatives","They inhibit T cells, mast cells and keratinocytes","They block histamine receptors within the skin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and they are second line after topical steroids. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What is mupirocin used to eliminate?","choices":["Pseudomonas colonisation of burns","Candida of the skin folds","Nasal carriage of Staphylococcus aureus","Dermatophyte infection of the feet"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it covers resistant strains particularly. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which retinoid is used for both acne and psoriasis?","choices":["Adapalene","Tazarotene","Alitretinoin","Bexarotene"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, a third generation retinoid sometimes combined with a topical steroid. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which Gram-positive organism is not covered by sulfamethoxazole/trimethoprim?","choices":["Enterococci","Staphylococcus aureus","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Listeria monocytogenes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, though it does cover resistant Staphylococcus aureus. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"What happens as cephalosporin generation advances?","choices":["More Gram-negative activity, less Gram-positive","More Gram-positive activity, less Gram-negative","Anaerobic activity increases with each step","Central nervous system penetration steadily falls"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, except that the fourth generation keeps both. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What is intrinsic sympathomimetic activity?","choices":["Complete blockade of both beta receptor types","Selective blockade of beta-1 receptors only","Added blockade of alpha-1 receptors","Weak agonist action alongside receptor blockade"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, useful where a blocker causes excessive bradycardia. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"Which electrolyte disturbances follow the echinocandins?","choices":["Hyperkalaemia and hypercalcaemia","Hypernatraemia and hyperchloraemia","Hypophosphataemia alone","Hypokalaemia and hypomagnesaemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, alongside tachycardia, headache and blood dyscrasias. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"Which organism colonises the acne follicle?","choices":["Staphylococcus aureus","Malassezia furfur","Propionibacterium acnes","Streptococcus pyogenes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, proliferating in the anaerobic conditions that pooled sebum creates. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which of these agents is bacteriostatic?","choices":["Vancomycin","Metronidazole","Tetracyclines","Aminoglycosides"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, along with erythromycin, sulfonamides, trimethoprim and clindamycin. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which aminopenicillin can only be given by mouth?","choices":["Ampicillin","Piperacillin","Nafcillin","Amoxicillin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ampicillin can be given intravenously or orally. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What is epinephrine reversal?","choices":["Beta effects are blocked, leaving the alpha action unopposed","Epinephrine is converted to norepinephrine","The drug reverses reuptake into the neuron","Alpha vasoconstriction is blocked, leaving beta vasodilation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, so blood pressure falls instead of rising. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"Who should avoid the topical immunomodulators?","choices":["Patients over sixty years of age","Patients with a history of asthma","Patients using sunscreen regularly","Patients with weakened immune systems"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, given the possible cancer risk. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"How does dopamine affect the renal circulation?","choices":["It constricts renal arteries at all doses","It dilates renal and splanchnic arteries","It has no effect on renal blood flow","It reduces glomerular filtration directly"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, through dopaminergic receptors. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"How does metronidazole damage bacteria?","choices":["It blocks DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV","It inhibits the 50S ribosomal subunit","It causes helical loss and DNA strand breakage","It prevents cell wall cross-linking"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, acting on anaerobes and parasites. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"What is the ciprofloxacin oral dose?","choices":["500 to 750 milligrams once daily","400 milligrams once daily","250 milligrams every 8 hours","500 to 750 milligrams every 12 hours"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with 400 milligrams every 12 hours intravenously. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Why should oxymetazoline not be used beyond three days?","choices":["It causes permanent loss of smell","It raises intraocular pressure dangerously","It causes nasal mucosal ulceration","Rebound congestion, rhinitis medicamentosa"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, as an alpha-1 and alpha-2 agonist decongestant. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"How does carbachol cause epinephrine release?","choices":["Through nicotinic action on the adrenal medulla","Through direct beta-1 receptor stimulation","By blocking norepinephrine reuptake","By displacing stored catecholamines"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, in addition to its muscarinic effects. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Through which receptor does epinephrine reduce insulin release?","choices":["Beta-1","Alpha-1","Beta-2","Alpha-2"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, while glycogenolysis and glucagon release run through beta-2. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"Which electrolyte disturbance does succinylcholine cause?","choices":["Hyperkalaemia","Hypokalaemia","Hypercalcaemia","Hyponatraemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, by increasing potassium release from intracellular stores. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"What is missing from clindamycin's spectrum?","choices":["Gram-positive anaerobes","Gram-negative anaerobes","Gram-negative aerobes","Resistant Staphylococcus aureus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It covers Gram-positive aerobes and anaerobes of both kinds. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"What is dopamine used to treat?","choices":["Chronic heart failure at home","Anaphylaxis in the community","Asthma exacerbation","Cardiogenic and septic shock"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with nausea, hypertension and arrhythmias as adverse effects. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"When is systemic rather than topical therapy indicated for acne?","choices":["Any disease lasting over a month","Only when scarring has occurred","Whenever comedones are present","Moderate to severe disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, while mild to moderate disease is treated topically. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which factor predisposes to fungal infection by removing competition?","choices":["Warm moist environments such as a nappy area","Antibiotic therapy","Diabetes as a metabolic abnormality","Burns and indwelling catheters"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, listed alongside loss of barriers and immunodeficiency. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"Which corticosteroid potency suits the face and infants?","choices":["Medium potency","Medium to high potency","Superpotent class 1 agents","Low potency"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it is also better for long-term therapy. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which central nervous system effects do the fluoroquinolones cause?","choices":["Seizures as the characteristic presentation","Hallucinations and vivid nightmares","Dizziness, insomnia and somnolence","Short-term memory blockade"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and confusion is a particular risk in the elderly. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which ganglionic blocker is not a competitive antagonist?","choices":["Hexamethonium","Mecamylamine","Trimethaphan","Nicotine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, since all the others are nondepolarising competitive agents. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which direct-acting cholinergic agonist is a naturally occurring alkaloid?","choices":["Bethanechol","Pilocarpine","Carbachol","Acetylcholine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, as against the choline esters such as bethanechol. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which effects are nicotinic rather than muscarinic?","choices":["Miosis, bradycardia, bronchospasm, salivation","Mydriasis, tachycardia, weakness, hypertension","Defecation, urination, emesis, lacrimation","Bronchorrhoea and diarrhoea"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with fasciculations completing the group. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which muscarinic receptors are functionally characterised?","choices":["M1 and M2 only","All five subclasses equally","M1, M2 and M3","M4 and M5"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, while M4 and M5 are described only on neurons. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"How does vancomycin differ mechanically from a penicillin?","choices":["It blocks the 50S ribosomal subunit instead","It binds two D-alanines rather than the enzyme","It disrupts the outer membrane as a detergent","It inhibits DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is a glycopeptide, not a beta-lactam. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which enzyme does trimethoprim inhibit?","choices":["Tetrahydropteroic acid synthetase","Thymidylate synthase directly","Dihydrofolate reductase","DNA gyrase in the folate pathway"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, while sulfamethoxazole blocks the step before it. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"What is edrophonium used to diagnose?","choices":["Alzheimer disease","Organophosphate poisoning","Glaucoma","Myasthenia gravis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, as the short-acting prototype inhibitor. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which term is synonymous with a cholinergic agonist?","choices":["Parasympatholytic","Parasympathomimetic","Antimuscarinic","Anticholinergic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, along with cholinomimetic. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which organisms cause superficial mycoses of hair and nails?","choices":["Aspergillus, Candida and Cryptococcus","Epidermophyton, Microsporum and Trichophyton","Blastomyces, Coccidioides and Histoplasma","Sporothrix and the chromomycosis agents"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, as opposed to the systemic and subcutaneous groups. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Why does phenylephrine cause reflex bradycardia?","choices":["It stimulates cardiac beta-1 receptors","It blocks sympathetic outflow centrally","It raises systolic and diastolic pressure","It acts directly on the sinoatrial node"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, as a selective alpha-1 agonist. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"Which organisms are covered poorly by the tetracyclines?","choices":["Pseudomonas and Clostridium difficile","Rickettsiae and Borrelia burgdorferi","Resistant Staphylococcus aureus","Treponema pallidum and Helicobacter pylori"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, while atypicals and animal-borne organisms are covered excellently. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which tinea infection is onychomycosis?","choices":["Tinea pedis","Tinea cruris","Tinea unguium","Tinea corporis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, affecting the nails. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Which vehicle should be avoided in intertriginous areas?","choices":["Lotion","Gel","Ointment","Cream"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because its occlusion is moderate to high. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Aminopenicillins are the drug of choice for which organisms?","choices":["Enterococcus and Listeria","Pseudomonas and Serratia","Bacteroides and Clostridium","Staphylococcus and Streptococcus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, along with endocarditis prophylaxis and upper respiratory infection. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Why are fungi resistant to antibiotics?","choices":["They are eukaryotic, with different ribosomes","They lack a cell wall for drugs to target","They grow too slowly for drugs to act","They produce beta-lactamase enzymes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and they have a distinct nuclear membrane. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"From which plant does atropine come?","choices":["Penicillium griseofulvum","Deadly nightshade, Atropa belladonna","Bacillus polymyxa","A soil Streptomycetaceae species"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, one of several plants of the Solanaceae family. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is the fungal cell wall made of?","choices":["Peptidoglycan","Ergosterol","Chitin","Lipopolysaccharide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, while the cell membrane uses ergosterol rather than cholesterol. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"How has aminoglycoside dosing changed?","choices":["From every 24 hours towards every 8 hours","From intravenous towards oral administration","From every 8 hours towards every 24 hours","From weight-based towards fixed dosing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, supported by the post-antibiotic effect. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Why were lipid formulations of amphotericin B developed?","choices":["To allow oral rather than intravenous dosing","To reduce toxicity, at 20 to 50 times the cost","To broaden the antifungal spectrum","To shorten the infusion below one hour"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, compared with the deoxycholate formulation. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"What must be adjusted when prescribing oseltamivir?","choices":["The dose, for hepatic function","The route, from oral to intravenous","The dose, for renal function","Nothing; it is given at a fixed dose"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it is available orally only. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 127"},{"q":"Why are cholinesterase inhibitors used in Alzheimer disease?","choices":["The disease involves loss of cholinergic neurons","They reverse the underlying amyloid deposition","They block excess acetylcholine centrally","They restore dopamine in the basal ganglia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, so these drugs slow progression. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Why does hyperthyroidism exaggerate epinephrine's effects?","choices":["Reduced hepatic clearance of the drug","Reduced monoamine oxidase activity","Increased catecholamine storage in nerves","Increased production of adrenergic receptors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, giving a hypersensitive response. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"What fraction of patients must stop intravenous ganciclovir for side effects?","choices":["About one third","About one in twenty","About two thirds","Almost none"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is what limits the intravenous route. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"Which cephalosporin is preferred in neonatal fever and sepsis?","choices":["Ceftriaxone","Cefotaxime","Cefazolin","Cefuroxime"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because ceftriaxone cannot be used in the first 30 days of life. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 34"}]};
